A||||8||manorris||stumbling on acceleration||||9||
Z||000000||manorris||12-26-2000||07:29 PM||||I have an 87 fiero 4 cyl. It has 160,000 miles and has been pretty good to me so far.<BR>But recently it has been acting up. In park and neutral I can rev up the engine to as high of rpms as I want without any problems. But when it is put in gear and I give it too much gas at once it begins to stumble, jerk, and backfire out the throttle body. It does ok if I only press the accelerator down a little, but too much and it is stumbling. This condition gets a lot worse as my car warms up. To the point where when it is fully warm it will stall out when I put it in gear. There are no codes being set and I can't figure it out. Any insight would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||9
Z||000001||88formula||12-26-2000||08:37 PM||||It’s just a guess but one of or both of your coils may be shot. It could also be ignition wires. To test this theory spray the ignition wires with soapy water in a spray bottle. If the engine starts to miss or stalls you have found the problem (leaking ignition). Try the coil also. The test works best when you put the engine under a load. Coils cost about $20 each and wires $35 for a set. Get the GM wires because most aftermarket wires are junk.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||12-26-2000||08:39 PM||||The same thing happened to mine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-26-2000||08:55 PM||||Check MAP and MAT. There are notes about both in my cave. Either one of those could cause this. Also check for vac leaks at base of TBI.<P><A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||12-26-2000||08:58 PM||||Low fuel pressure will also do weird stuff. Ever replace the filter?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-26-2000||09:36 PM||||Check those plug wires. <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||HAGS||12-27-2000||12:21 AM||||You described the problem im having perfectly! Except on top of that smoke comes out of the TBI and its knocking... I like the replies to yours better then mine, but I didnt describe it as good as you, my thread is "Problems with TBI", I took out the TBI and pulled all the pushrods, cleaned up the TBI but am going to replace it and none of the rods were bent.<p>[This message has been edited by HAGS (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||12-27-2000||09:22 AM||||<B>DO NOT pull on the plug wires of a running DIS fired engine!</B><P>1. The DIS ignition puts out nearly 40kV at over twice the current of HEI. (HEI makes about 30-32kV) DIS can kill you.<P>2. IF a wire comes off a DIS coil then it can arc back into the ignition module and fry it. The ignition module is over $120us.<P>If you think the plug wires are flaky just replace them. Borg Warner makes very nice ones that don't cost much.<P>After reading this thread again.... you need to replace the MAT. Backfires up the intake manifold will damage it. <P>Also check the PCV line intake backfires can blow the rubber elbow loose/off or damage it.<P>If the TPS has never been replaced do so. It's likely getting worn out. It could be all or part of the problem.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||12-27-2000||10:10 AM||||manorris,<P>I think your problems are typical.  Virtually everyone that I know (except theogre... because he is meticulous with maintenance of the DIS) has a similar problem with the DIS when it gets cold and or wet.  The easiest solution is to let the car warm up for a few minutes before driving.  I have been doing this for a couple of years now.  When it first happened, I replaced nearly every sensor back there, plugs & wires, reprogrammed the ECM... twice, replaced the ECM all to no avail.  I have had no problems for the last 40K letting it warm up.  <P><BR>theogre,<P>I thought you had done some research on the duke regarding the TBI and coolant lines (or something like that).  I could be mistaken, but I was under the impression that you decided that the Duke would never be an excellent "cold-running" engine.  Please correct me if I am wrong.  The conversation I am trying to recall took place anywhere from 1.5-1 year ago here on the Forum.  <P><BR>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
